nodecode-agentplaybooks is a rewrite of matebenyovszky's matebenyovszky/agentplaybooks:

  https://github.com/matebenyovszky/agentplaybooks
  commit f31e9c3af6520cebda1c0712447b4b487ffc12a7
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Máté Benyovszky (LICENSE)

The rewritten plugin is packages/hermes-memory/agentplaybooks of that
commit. Its six tools keep their names, descriptions and parameters
(apb_memory_search, apb_memory_read, apb_memory_write, apb_memory_archive,
apb_memory_delete, apb_memory_history); its wire keeps its shapes (GET
/api/playbooks/<guid>/memory with search=, key= and history_key=; PUT and
DELETE on /api/playbooks/<guid>/memory/<key>; X-API-Key; the anonymous
metadata GET that a private playbook answers 404 to); and its behaviour is
carried over: the fail-closed visibility check before every write, the
visibility and URL rules (https only, http only on localhost, no credentials
or query in the base URL, the GUID validated as hex), the tier set, the
provenance a write carries, the archive that preserves value and memory time,
the read-only shared playbooks and writable team playbooks each with their
own key, and the bounded recall (four keywords, eight playbooks, four
seconds, eight records, 8,000 characters) whose result is injected as
reference data.

What was left out, and why: mirroring of the harness's own memory files,
because Hermes's MEMORY.md and USER.md have no Nodecode twin to mirror;
the primary-agent write gate and the session-switch/author bookkeeping of a
gateway's participants, which a Nodecode session does not have; and the
plugin's hermes memory setup/status commands, whose answers the section,
/settings and /agentplaybooks give. The recall runs as one sequential pass
under the same four-second budget instead of the plugin's four workers.
LICENSE is the plugin folder's LICENSE, unchanged.
